As a Premier League club not involved in any [[UEFA club competitions|UEFA competitions]], Newcastle United entered the cup in the second round where they were drawn away against fellow [[Premier League]] club [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]]. The match was played at the [[City Ground]] on 28 August 2024, where Newcastle won 4–3 on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]] following a 1–1 draw after a goal for Newcastle from [[Joe Willock]] and a goal for Forest from [[Jota Silva]]. [[Callum Hudson-Odoi]], [[Nikola Milenković]] and [[Neco Williams]] all scored their spot kicks for Forest, however, [[Ibrahim Sangaré (Ivorian footballer)|Ibrahim Sangaré]] and [[Taiwo Awoniyi]] missed their penalties for the home side. [[Alexander Isak]], [[Bruno Guimarães]], [[Anthony Gordon (footballer)|Anthony Gordon]] and [[Sean Longstaff]] converted their penalties with [[Joelinton]] the only player to miss theirs for the visitors. <ref>{{cite news |title=Nottingham Forest vs Newcastle United: League Cup |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cj35z81mmelt |work=BBC Sport |date=28 August 2024 |access-date=6 February 2025 }}</ref> In the third round, they were drawn against [[EFL League Two]] club [[AFC Wimbledon|Wimbledon]], played at [[St James' Park]] on 1 October. The match finished 1–0, with Newcastle's winning goal coming from a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] by defender [[Fabian Schär]] in the first minute of first-half injury time.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Abraham |first1=Timothy |title=EFL Cup: Newcastle United v AFC Wimbledon |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/c9vpme1enrvt |work=BBC Sport |date=1 October 2024 |access-date=6 February 2025 }}</ref> The fixture was initially scheduled to be hosted by Wimbledon on 24 September, but was postponed and switched to Newcastle due to flooding at Wimbledon's stadium, [[Plough Lane]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Postponed Wimbledon Carabao Cup tie to be played at St James' Park |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cpqz28rgwqlo |publisher=BBC Sport |date=23 September 2024 |access-date=6 February 2025 }}</ref> In the fourth round, Newcastle were drawn at home against Premier League club and [[2024 EFL Cup final|2024 finalists]] [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], with the match played at St James' Park on 30 October. The match saw Newcastle defeat Chelsea 2–0, with goals coming from both Isak and an [[Own goal#Association football|own goal]] from Chelsea defender [[Axel Disasi]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Brotherton |first1=Alex |title=Newcastle United 2–0 Chelsea: Alexander Isak scores as hosts claim impressive win |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/c9dllj2ld85t |work=BBC Sport |date=30 October 2024 |access-date=6 February 2025 }}</ref> 

In the quarter-finals, Newcastle were drawn at home to Premier League club [[Brentford F.C.|Brentford]], with the match played at St James' Park on 18 December. Two goals from [[Sandro Tonali]] inspired Newcastle to a 3–1 victory, with Schär also getting on the scoresheet, as they booked a place in the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sutcliffe |first1=Steve |title=Newcastle United 3–1 Brentford: Newcastle cruise into Carabao Cup semis |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cdekddne4edt |work=BBC Sport |date=18 December 2024 |access-date=6 February 2025 }}</ref> There, they would face Premier League club [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] over two legs, with the first leg played away at the [[Emirates Stadium]] on 7 January 2025. Newcastle beat the Gunners 2–0, with goals from Isak and Gordon giving the Magpies a two-goal advantage in the second leg.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Emons |first1=Michael |title=Arsenal vs Newcastle LIVE |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/ckg9dgm5gk4t |work=BBC Sport |date=7 January 2025 |access-date=6 February 2025 }}</ref> This was played at St James' Park on 5 February, where Newcastle repeated the first leg scoreline with a 2–0 victory, courtesy of goals from Gordon and [[Jacob Murphy]], as Newcastle won the semi-final 4–0 on aggregate to reach their second final in three seasons.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McNulty |first1=Phil |title=Newcastle 2–0 (4–0 agg) Arsenal: Magpies thrash Gunners over two legs to make Carabao Cup final |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/c1we9vxz7xdt |work=BBC Sport |date=5 February 2025 |access-date=6 February 2025 }}</ref>

==Pre-match==

=== Background ===
This was Newcastle's third appearance in the final, having previously lost in 1976 and 2023. Liverpool were the reigning EFL Cup holders and were appearing in a record fifteenth final, having won the cup a record 10 times.<ref name="RSSSF" />

Newcastle were aiming to end one of the longest trophy droughts in English football,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Last Major Trophy Won by English Clubs from 1874-2022 |url=https://www.myfootballfacts.com/england_footy/english-domestic/last-major-trophy-won-by-english-clubs/ |access-date=23 February 2023 |website=My Football Facts}}</ref> with their last major honour being the [[1968–69 Inter-Cities Fairs Cup]]. Their most recent major domestic trophy was even earlier, when they won the [[1954–55 FA Cup]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.football365.com/news/the-big-sixs-longest-trophy-droughts-and-how-they-ended|title=Big Six longest trophy droughts and how they eventually ended as Spurs edge towards 15 years|work=Football365.com|first=Ian|last=Watson|date=10 October 2022}}</ref>

=== Team news ===
[[Anthony Gordon (footballer)|Anthony Gordon]] missed the final for Newcastle after receiving a red card in an [[2024–25 FA Cup|FA Cup]] defeat against [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton and Hove Albion]] for a challenge on [[Jan Paul van Hecke]] on 2 March.<ref name=":0">{{cite news |title=Anthony Gordon: Newcastle winger to miss Carabao Cup final with Lewis Hall out for season through injury |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11095/13321132/anthony-gordon-newcastle-winger-to-miss-carabao-cup-final-after-club-decide-not-to-appeal-red-card-against-brighton |work=Sky Sports |date=5 March 2025 |access-date=5 March 2025 }}</ref> [[Lewis Hall (footballer)|Lewis Hall]] and [[Sven Botman]] would also miss the final with Hall out for the remainder of the season with a foot injury and Botman ruled out for eight weeks with another knee injury.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hall to miss rest of the season with foot injury |publisher=Newcastle United |url=https://www.newcastleunited.com/en/news/hall-to-miss-rest-of-the-season-with-foot-injury |access-date=2025-03-10 |website=newcastleunited.com }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-03-07 |title=Newcastle United defender Sven Botman ruled out for eight weeks with knee injury |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/c5y0py775kxo |access-date=2025-03-10 |website=BBC Sport }}</ref> Five of Newcastle's starting line-up had started the 2023 final, while two had appeared off the bench. Callum Wilson, who appeared off the bench, had started the 2023 final and Joe Willock played as a substitute on both occasions.  For Liverpool, [[Trent Alexander-Arnold]], [[Conor Bradley]], [[Joe Gomez]], and [[Tyler Morton]] all missed the final due to injury.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-03-14 |title=Injured Alexander-Arnold out of League Cup final |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/c778ex3278zo |access-date=2025-03-14 |website=BBC Sport }}</ref>

Newcastle were the first side to win the cup with as many as six English starters in the final since [[Leicester City]] in the [[1997 Football League Cup final]]. Runners-up [[Bradford City A.F.C.|Bradford City]] were the last team to field that many English starters in a final, in the [[2013 Football League Cup final]].

==Match==

=== Summary ===
In the final minute of the first half, [[Dan Burn]] scored with a header to the left corner of the net after a corner from the left from [[Kieran Trippier]] to give Newcastle a 1–0 lead at half-time.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.theguardian.com/football/2025/mar/16/newcastle-sink-liverpool-to-savour-taste-of-glory-after-decades-of-drought|title=Newcastle sink Liverpool to savour taste of glory after decades of drought|date=16 March 2025|work=Guardian|accessdate=19 March 2025}}</ref> In the 52nd minute, [[Alexander Isak]] scored with a half-volley low to the left of the net to give Newcastle a 2–0 lead. {{external media | float = right | width = 300px | video1 = [https://x.com/NUFC/status/1903018243859603955 Second goal of the match.]<ref name=secondGoal>{{Cite tweet |user=NUFC |number=1903018243859603955 |title=Limbs}}</ref>}} In the fourth minute of time added on Liverpool substitute [[Federico Chiesa]] scored with a low left footed finish to make it 2–1. The match eventually ended as a 2–1 victory for Newcastle.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-03-16 |title=Liverpool vs. Newcastle final score: Carabao Cup final result, stats as Burn and Isak secure historic Wembley triumph {{!}} Sporting News United Kingdom |url=https://www.sportingnews.com/uk/football/news/liverpool-newcastle-score-result-updates-stats-carabao-cup-final/4c091cf42772a67fc3461a36 |access-date=2025-03-17 |website=www.sportingnews.com |language=en-gb}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Liverpool 1–2 Newcastle: Dan Burn, Alexander Isak goals deliver Toon Carabao Cup win to end 70-year trophy wait |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11938/13327769/liverpool-1-2-newcastle-dan-burn-alexander-isak-goals-deliver-toon-carabao-cup-win-to-end-70-year-trophy-wait |access-date=2025-03-17 |website=Sky Sports |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Liverpool 1–2 Newcastle: Magpies win Carabao Cup to end 70-year domestic trophy wait |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/live/c62zl58dg8qt |access-date=2025-03-17 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref>

